original_raw_output:
Here's an example showing improper vs. proper use of Java's Path API for file access:

```java
import java.nio.file.Path;
import java.nio.file.Paths;
import java.io.File;

public class FileAccessExample {
    public void readUserFile(String userInput) {
        // VULNERABLE: Direct use of user input in path construction
        // allows path traversal attacks using "../" sequences
        Path filePath = Paths.get("/home/app/users/" + userInput);
        File userFile = filePath.toFile();

        if (userFile.exists()) {
            // Process the file...
            System.out.println("Reading file: " + userFile.getAbsolutePath());
        }
    }
}
```
***
```java
import java.nio.file.Path;
import java.nio.file.Paths;
import java.io.File;

public class FileAccessExample {
    public void readUserFile(String userInput) {
        // Sanitize user input by removing path traversal sequences
        String sanitizedInput = userInput.replaceAll("\\.\\./", "")
                                       .replaceAll("\\\\", "");

        // Normalize the path and verify it's within allowed directory
        Path basePath = Paths.get("/home/app/users/");
        Path filePath = basePath.resolve(sanitizedInput).normalize();

        // Check if the resolved path is actually within the base directory
        if (!filePath.startsWith(basePath)) {
            throw new SecurityException("Access denied: Invalid path");
        }

        File userFile = filePath.toFile();
        if (userFile.exists()) {
            // Process the file...
            System.out.println("Reading file: " + userFile.getAbsolutePath());
        }
    }
}
```

raw_output:
Here's an example showing improper string comparison in a login authentication system:

```java
// LoginService.java
public class LoginService {
    // vulnerable: using == for string comparison can lead to authentication bypass
    public boolean authenticateUser(String username, String password) {
        String validUsername = "admin";
        String validPassword = "secretpass123";

        // Using == compares object references, not string contents
        if (username == validUsername && password == validPassword) {
            return true;
        }
        return false;
    }
}
```
***
```java
// LoginService.java
public class LoginService {
    // Fixed: using equals() method for proper string content comparison
    public boolean authenticateUser(String username, String password) {
        String validUsername = "admin";
        String validPassword = "secretpass123";

        // equals() compares the actual contents of the strings
        if (validUsername.equals(username) && validPassword.equals(password)) {
            return true;
        }
        return false;
    }
}
```

